# Cloud

## What I am learning

Cloud infrastructure as a set of transferable concepts: compute, networking, storage, managed databases, identity and access, load balancing, autoscaling, monitoring, and cost. The platforms used for practice are AWS and Azure, but the priority is understanding what each service does and why, not memorizing console buttons.

## Why it matters

Most production systems run in the cloud, and most cloud incidents come from a few causes: networking misconfiguration, overly broad permissions, and unexpected cost. The Linux, Networking, and Docker topics all apply directly: a virtual machine is a Linux server, a security group is a firewall, and a container service runs the images built in the pipeline.

> **Goal:** understand cloud infrastructure well enough to deploy, secure, and operate the main project, and to transfer that knowledge between providers.

---

## Learning objectives

By the end of this topic I can:

- Explain the cloud service models (IaaS, PaaS, SaaS) and the shared responsibility model
- Create and connect to a virtual machine, and explain what I am paying for
- Design a network with a virtual network, public and private subnets, and routing
- Control traffic with security groups or firewalls and explain least privilege for network access
- Explain object, block, and file storage and when each is used
- Use a managed database and explain what the provider handles and what I still own
- Manage identity and access: users, roles, policies, and least privilege
- Put a load balancer in front of an application and configure health checks
- Configure DNS and TLS certificates for a public endpoint
- Explain autoscaling and what it needs to work (stateless services, good health checks)
- Monitor resources and set alerts
- Estimate, track, and reduce cost
- Explain which concepts are the same on AWS and Azure and which names differ

---

## Weekly progression

| Week | Focus | AWS / Azure examples |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | Cloud concepts, account setup, billing alerts, and IAM basics | IAM / Entra ID and RBAC |
| 2 | Compute: virtual machines, SSH access, and instance sizing | EC2 / Virtual Machines |
| 3 | Networking: virtual networks, subnets, routing, security groups, and NAT | VPC / Virtual Network, security groups / NSGs |
| 4 | Storage and managed databases | S3 / Blob Storage, RDS / Azure Database for PostgreSQL |
| 5 | Load balancing, DNS, TLS, and autoscaling | ALB / Application Gateway, Route 53 / Azure DNS |
| 6 | Deploy the main project (Stage 5) end to end | Container service or VM-based deployment |
| 7 | Monitoring, alerting, cost control, and cleanup | CloudWatch / Azure Monitor, cost tools |
| 8 | Mini-project, review, and a provider comparison | |

This topic delivers Stage 5 of the main project: internet, load balancer, application, database, and cache.

