## Checklist

- [ ] Explain IaaS, PaaS, and SaaS
- [ ] Explain the shared responsibility model
- [ ] Set up billing alerts before creating any resource
- [ ] Create a user or role with least-privilege permissions, and never use the root account for daily work
- [ ] Launch a virtual machine and connect with SSH keys
- [ ] Explain virtual network, subnet, route table, and gateway
- [ ] Explain public versus private subnets
- [ ] Write security group rules that open only what is needed
- [ ] Explain NAT and why private resources still reach the internet
- [ ] Explain object versus block storage
- [ ] Create a managed database in a private subnet
- [ ] Explain what a managed database handles (backups, patching) and what stays my job
- [ ] Configure a load balancer with health checks
- [ ] Point a DNS name at the application and enable HTTPS
- [ ] Explain what autoscaling needs in order to work
- [ ] Set up monitoring and an alert
- [ ] Read a cost report and find the most expensive resource
- [ ] Tear down everything and confirm nothing is still billing
- [ ] Map one service from AWS to its Azure equivalent

A box is ticked only when I can do or explain the task without relying entirely on a tutorial.

---

## Safety rules for cloud practice

Cloud mistakes can cost money or expose data, so these rules apply to every lab:

```text
1. Set a billing alert and a budget before creating anything.
2. Never use the root or global admin account for daily work. Enable MFA on it.
3. Never commit access keys. Prefer roles and short-lived credentials.
4. Never open a database or SSH to the whole internet (0.0.0.0/0).
5. Tag every resource with the lab name so it can be found and deleted.
6. Tear down at the end of every session and verify.
```

---

## Exercises

- [ ] Create a budget alert and a least-privilege user, then test what that user cannot do
- [ ] Launch a VM, SSH in, install a service, and reach it from outside
- [ ] Draw a network diagram of a public subnet and a private subnet, then build it
- [ ] Restrict SSH to my own IP and verify that other addresses are blocked
- [ ] Upload a file to object storage and control who can read it
- [ ] Connect the backend to a managed database that has no public address
- [ ] Put two instances behind a load balancer and watch traffic alternate
- [ ] Build the same VM on both AWS and Azure and write down the naming differences

## Labs

Each lab ends with a written note: symptom, evidence, hypothesis, root cause, fix, lesson.

- [ ] **Cannot SSH to the VM:** check the key, security group, route, and the instance state, one layer at a time (linking to Networking)
- [ ] **App reachable by IP but not by name:** diagnose DNS
- [ ] **Backend cannot reach the managed database:** find whether it is the security group, subnet, or credentials
- [ ] **Load balancer reports unhealthy targets:** find the health check or binding mistake
- [ ] **Permission denied:** a role cannot do what it needs, fix the policy without making it broader than necessary
- [ ] **Surprise bill:** find which resource caused a cost spike and prevent it

## Mini-project: Deploy the main project to the cloud

The main project running publicly, with:

- A public load balancer with HTTPS, and a DNS name
- The application running in private subnets
- A managed PostgreSQL database that is not publicly accessible, and a cache
- Least-privilege roles and no secrets in the repository
- Security groups that allow only the required traffic
- Monitoring and at least two alerts
- A documented architecture diagram and a cost estimate
- A teardown procedure that was tested
- A short comparison of how the same design maps to AWS and Azure
- An ADR for the deployment model (VM, container service, or other) and the provider

